      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the coordinates of point \\( T(r, s) \\) on the line segment \\( PQ \\), and then find \\( r+s \\). Let's go through the steps in detail.\n\n1. **Find the coordinates of \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\):**\n   \n   - The point \\( P \\) is where the line \\( y = -\\frac{3}{4}x + 9 \\) intersects the \\( x \\)-axis. At the \\( x \\)-axis, \\( y = 0 \\). So, we set \\( y = 0 \\) and solve for \\( x \\):\n     \\[\n     0 = -\\frac{3}{4}x + 9 \\implies \\frac{3}{4}x = 9 \\implies x = 12.\n     \\]\n     Therefore, \\( P = (12, 0) \\).\n   \n   - The point \\( Q \\) is where the line \\( y = -\\frac{3}{4}x + 9 \\) intersects the \\( y \\)-axis. At the \\( y \\)-axis, \\( x = 0 \\). So, we set \\( x = 0 \\) and solve for \\( y \\):\n     \\[\n     y = -\\frac{3}{4}(0) + 9 = 9.\n     \\]\n     Therefore, \\( Q = (0, 9) \\).\n\n2. **Calculate the area of \\( \\triangle POQ \\):**\n   \n   The area of \\( \\triangle POQ \\) is a triangle with vertices at \\( O = (0, 0) \\), \\( P = (12, 0) \\), and \\( Q = (0, 9) \\). The base \\( OQ \\) is along the \\( y \\)-axis from \\( y = 0 \\) to \\( y = 9 \\), which is 9 units. The height \\( OP \\) is along the \\( x \\)-axis from \\( x = 0 \\) to \\( x = 12 \\), which is 12 units. The area \\( A_{POQ} \\) of this triangle is:\n     \\[\n     A_{POQ} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 12 \\times 9 = 54.\n     \\]\n\n3. **Determine the area of \\( \\triangle TOP \\):**\n   \n   The area of \\( \\triangle TOP \\) is a triangle with vertices at \\( O = (0, 0) \\), \\( P = (12, 0) \\), and \\( T = (r, s) \\). Since \\( T \\) is on the line segment \\( PQ \\), we can use the fact that the area of a triangle is proportional to the length of the base when the height is constant. The base \\( OT \\) is the \\( x \\)-coordinate of \\( T \\), which is \\( r \\), and the height is the \\( y \\)-coordinate of \\( T \\), which is \\( s \\). The ratio of the areas of \\( \\triangle TOP \\) to \\( \\triangle POQ \\) is the same as the ratio of the lengths of \\( OT \\) to \\( OP \\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\text{Area of } \\triangle TOP}{\\text{Area of } \\triangle POQ} = \\frac{r}{12}.\n     \\]\n   Given that the area of \\( \\triangle POQ \\) is three times the area of \\( \\triangle TOP \\), we have:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{r}{12} = 3 \\implies r = 36.\n     \\]\n\n4. **Calculate \\( r + s \\):**\n   \n   Since \\( T = (r, s) \\) and we have \\( r = 36 \\), we need to find \\( s \\). The coordinates of \\( T \\) must satisfy the equation of the line \\( y = -\\frac{3}{4}x + 9 \\):\n     \\[\n     s = -\\frac{3}{4}(36) + 9 = -27 + 9 = -18.\n     \\]\n     Therefore, \\( r + s = 36 + (-18) = 18 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{18}\n\\]",
